`,s.jsx(P,{items:[{q:"Why does 'holding others constant' make a coefficient's sign flip when you add a predictor?",a:"Because the coefficient answers a different question in each model. Alone, a predictor absorbs the effect of everything correlated with it; once a confounder is included, the coefficient measures only the part of the relationship not explained by that confounder. A sign flip is a signal of confounding, not an error."},{q:"Residuals fan out as fitted values grow. Name the violated assumption and say what is and is not broken.",a:"Homoscedasticity. Point predictions and coefficient estimates remain unbiased; what breaks is the STANDARD ERRORS, which are too narrow at high fitted values — so confidence intervals and p-values are wrong. Fixes: weighted least squares, a log transform, or HC1/HC3 robust standard errors."},{q:"You add five junk predictors and R^2 rises. Explain, and give the right metric.",a:"R^2 is a monotone function of residual sum of squares, and adding any column can only reduce RSS by fitting noise — it can never fall. Adjusted R^2 penalises by the parameter count, so it goes DOWN when a predictor buys less than its degrees of freedom cost."},{q:"Residuals-vs-fitted looks clean, but residuals-vs-age is U-shaped. Why did the first plot miss it?",a:"Fitted values are a blend of all predictors, so if size and location dominate y-hat, age's curvature is smeared across the x-axis and averages away. Always plot residuals against EACH predictor separately — that is what isolates a single feature's misspecification."},{q:"A point has Cook's distance 1.8. Delete it?",a:"Not automatically. High influence means the point moves the fit a lot, not that it is wrong. If it is a legitimate member of the population you are modelling, keep it — deleting it makes the model lie about the real data. Remove or model separately only if it belongs to a different population than the one you are predicting for, or use robust regression."}]}),`
